# the argument of kant-practical-1.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
# Main line: mathematics contradicts a purely empirical reason, so we are driven to doubt, and universal empiricism is shown to be absolute scepticism.
# S4 qualifies how far this applies to Hume; it is kept as premises and is not used for C.
atom	antinomy	mathematics proves the infinite divisibility of space, which empiricism cannot admit
atom	contra	this science (mathematics) is in contradiction with a reason that admits only empirical principles
atom	clash	the greatest possible evidence of demonstration is in manifest contradiction with the alleged conclusions from experience
atom	driven_doubt	we are driven to ask, like Cheselden's blind patient, which deceives me, sight or touch
atom	emp_felt	empiricism is based on a necessity felt
atom	rat_seen	rationalism is based on a necessity seen
atom	skeptic	universal empiricism reveals itself as absolute scepticism
atom	hume_skeptic	absolute scepticism is rightly attributed to Hume in an unqualified sense
atom	touchstone	Hume left at least one certain touchstone
atom	apriori_only	such a touchstone can only be found in a priori principles
atom	exp_judgements	experience consists not only of feelings but also of judgements
P1	S1	antinomy	kind empirical
P2	S1	antinomy → contra
P3	S1	contra → clash ∧ driven_doubt
P4	S2	emp_felt ∧ rat_seen	kind conceptual
C1	S1	clash ∧ driven_doubt	from P1 P2 P3
A1	-	driven_doubt → skeptic
C	S3	skeptic	from C1 A1
P5	S4	touchstone	kind empirical
P6	S4	touchstone → apriori_only
P7	S4	touchstone → ¬hume_skeptic
P8	S4	exp_judgements
C2	S4	¬hume_skeptic	from P5 P7
